[module-registry name=cx-modules
  [doc [#
THE MODULE REGISTRY — one row per shipped surface, and the single place its
RING and its GROUP are DECLARED (RULED: 1427-f, OL-15, RS-1).

TWO RINGS, THEN GROUPS (RULED: RS-1). Ring 0 is the data format and Ring 1
the language; they are the only rings — frozen, changed by decision, each an
import contract that buys a product property (Ring 0 cannot execute; Ring 1
cannot reach a socket, a store or a protocol). "Ring 2" and "Ring 3" left the
vocabulary. Everything else is a GROUP — platform, binding, ecosystem, dist —
whose import graph is declared per module and must be acyclic, and a module
may import the rings and whatever its manifest declares. The one group this
file carries is `platform`: a platform row reads `ring=1 group=platform` (its
ring is the ring of its highest verb, 0 or 1; the group is what says it may
import the platform graph), and `placement-gate` refuses a `ring=2` by name.

Before this file a surface's placement was stated in four places by
implication — the spec's directory, the corpus's `ring=` header, the
directory its V code sits in (`vcx/code` = the rings, a product's
`vcx/<vmodule>/` = the platform, RULED: RS-24),
and its `[?lib]` namespace — and nothing compared them. #1077 and OL-14
record what that costs. This file is the declaration; `make placement-gate`
(scripts/placement_gate.cx) refuses a tree where any of the four disagrees
with the row, or where an artifact under a ring directory has no row.

Derived FROM this file: the bundled-surface census asserted in
vcx/tests/stdlib_umbrella_test.v, the namespace migration tool
(`cx --migrate-namespace registry/modules.cxd`), and — as #1427 proceeds —
the catalog step and the two READMEs.

TODAY AND THE DESTINATION. Every path column names where the artifact IS,
on the tree this file sits in. A `<column>-to` names where a move PUTS it,
and exists only while that move is still owed: writing one is how a row says
"this dimension has not moved yet", and deleting it is how the commit that
moves the artifact closes that line. So the check passes on today's tree
without being vacuous — a dimension with no `-to` is held to its ring on
every run — and the set of `-to` values IS the census of what the move still
owes. A `-to` equal to its column, or naming a path that already exists, is
refused: a stale declaration would hide exactly the drift this file exists
to name.

#1427's cutover is SPENT: no row carries a `-to` or an `ns-to` any more,
and the census the check prints reads zero on every dimension but one: five
rows carry a `suite-ring` again since the x/ graduation (RULED: RS-4, RS-1 —
D45a) — their suites grade in lane ring=1 while their group's lane is 2 —
so "5 corpus-header ring" is owed, by the repository that owns the headers.
The columns stay because the mechanism is not the cutover — the
next decision that moves an artifact writes one, and the same two refusals
keep it from going stale.

NOT WRITTEN YET is a different statement from NOT MOVED YET, and it is
`status=planned`, never a `-to`. A planned row's columns name where the
artifact WILL be, the check holds it to its ring and its directories but not
to existence, and it refuses the row once every artifact is on disk — so the
declaration cannot outlive the writing. Six rows are planned today: `graphql`,
`soap`, `ftp`, `sftp` and `ws` (#1430 and its transports, and #1086's frame
codec beneath `kind=stream`) and `sync` (#1434) — each spec-first by its own
decision, none of them #1427's debt. `connector` was a seventh until its code
landed and its row read `current`.

COLUMNS
  name    the surface's call-site prefix — `[?lib '<ns>/<name>']` binds it.
          Unique per namespace, not globally: `term` is both a natives
          family (ns=none) and a Ring-1 module (ns=cx-stdlib)
  ring    0 = a surface synthesized by the codec registry (codec.md §3),
          1 = the language: pure/local, or a platform row's ring. The rings
          are 0 and 1 only (RULED: RS-1); `ring=2` is refused by name. This
          is the DECLARED ring — the decision, not a location, so it has no
          `-to`: what moves is the artifact, never the ruling
  group   `platform` = the module serves or reaches a store or a protocol, so
          it may import the platform graph above the rings (RULED: RS-1).
          Absent means no group: the module imports only the rings. A
          group=platform row sits in the platform directories
          (spec/03-approved/platform/, conformance/platform/, its product's
          vcx/<vmodule>/ -- registry/repos.cxd names it, RULED: RS-24)
          whatever its ring, and its corpus header grades in the platform
          lane (`ring=2` — the CORPUS's attribute, the lane that grades the
          suite, which RS-1 does not rename). Namespace and group agree:
          `cx-stdlib` ↔ no group, `cx-platform` ↔ `group=platform`, `none`
          ↔ either
  ns      the resolver namespace TODAY: `cx-stdlib` or `cx-platform` (RS-4
          retired the two whole-name namespaces `cx-xap` and `cx-fabric`, and
          RS-31's D63a the `cx-x` experimental tier, which had no member
          left; placement-gate refuses a row naming any of them). `none` = no
          `[?lib]` surface at all (natives, backend engines, design texts)
  ns-to   the namespace 1427-b moves it to (`cx-platform` for every
          group=platform module; nothing else is renamed)
  kind    module   a `[?lib]`-resolvable module
          natives  a dispatch family with no module surface
          backend  an external engine reached through core verbs
          design   an approved design text with no surface of its own
          codec    a surface synthesized by the codec registry
          suite    fixtures whose parent spec is not a module of its own
  spec    the normative text
  corpus  the graded fixtures
  source  the bundled CX source (stdlib/*.cx, or the pinned deps/<repo>/… one
          of a `repo=` row), or `none`
  repo    the REPOSITORY that owns the artifacts, when it is not this one
          (RULED: RS-7, RS-12). A module extracted into its own repository
          keeps its row here — the binary still ships the surface — and its
          spec, corpus and source columns name the pinned checkout
          `deps/<repo>/…` that `deps.cxd` pins and `make deps-sync` fetches.
          Absent means the artifacts are tracked in this repository, which is
          every other row. `placement-gate` refuses a `repo=` row whose path
          does not begin `deps/<repo>/`, and reads the ring of what follows
          the prefix, so an extraction cannot turn a ring check vacuous
  code    the V file(s) carrying the dispatch, or `none` for a pure-CX
          module — every one under the row's own directory (vcx/code for
          the rings, the product's vcx/<vmodule>/ for group=platform), or, on a `repo=` row,
          the pinned deps/<repo>/vcx/<vmodule>/ files — a module whose
          dispatch lives in a product is reached through code's
          builtin-dispatch registry, which the product's own init() fills,
          and never by name from vcx/code (RULED: D71a)
  half    a DECLARED Ring-1 half of a group=platform module (1427-d): the pure codec
          or client a Ring-1 consumer may need for profile composition. It
          stays in vcx/code and is named HERE, so the placement check reads
          it as declared rather than as a ring violation. The split trigger
          is the first Ring-1 CONSUMER of a half — that is how `http-client`
          became its own Ring-1 module (1427-e)
  suite-ring
          the `ring=` the corpus header carries TODAY, when it disagrees
          with the row's lane (its ring, or 2 for group=platform). Same
          contract as a `-to`: it exists only while the
          disagreement does, and the check refuses one that is already
          resolved
  status  current  the artifacts are on this tree
          planned  the row is DECLARED ahead of the artifacts: its columns
                   name where they will be written, and the decision that
                   owes them is named in the row's comment. Held to its ring
                   and its directories, not to existence — and REFUSED once
                   every artifact exists, which is the commit that flips it
                   to current
                   A planned row may carry `repo=`: the repository the
                   module lands in when it ships, its columns then naming
                   deps/<repo>/ paths; `deps-present` skips planned rows
                   (RULED: D72a)
          incoming the artifacts arrive with another branch's merge; the row
                   is here so that merge does not fail this check. Graded
                   when present, not required

MEMBERSHIP TEST (spec/03-approved/core/cx_partition.md §10, carried from
ledger/partition_I3_ring12_split.md, read through RS-1): a module that
imports the store or a protocol module, or that SERVES, is in the platform
group (`ring=1 group=platform`); otherwise it is Ring 1 with no group. A
module lives in the ring of its HIGHEST verb; a pure half a Ring-1 consumer
needs becomes its own Ring-1 module.
#]]

  [; ── The two catalogs (RULED: 1427-a, 1427-g; RS-1) — Ring 1's and the
       platform group's. A catalog directory's README is not a surface, so no
       `[module]` row can claim it; it is declared here so the placement
       check reads it as the catalog rather than as an unclaimed spec, and so
       a catalog directory can never lose the one page that enumerates it. ── ]
  [catalog ring=1 path=spec/03-approved/stdlib/README.md]
  [catalog ring=1 group=platform path=spec/03-approved/platform/README.md]

  [; ── The platform group's composition page (RULED: COMP-1). It declares no surface —
       no namespace, no corpus, no code — so no `[module]` row can claim it
       either, and it is declared here for the same reason the two READMEs
       are: `placement-gate` must read it as a claimed catalog page rather
       than as an unclaimed spec under a ring directory. See
       spec/03-approved/platform/composition.md §0. ────────────────────── ]
  [catalog ring=1 group=platform path=spec/03-approved/platform/composition.md]

  [; ── The platform group's deployment-topology page (RULED: COMP-2). Beside the
       composition page and declared for the same reason: it states where the
       platform's runtimes run and what carries a seam between two of them,
       declares no surface, and is claimed here as a catalog page rather than
       read by `placement-gate` as an unclaimed spec. See
       spec/03-approved/platform/deployment-topology.md §0. ───────────────── ]
  [catalog ring=1 group=platform path=spec/03-approved/platform/deployment-topology.md]

  [; ── The platform group's file-surface contract (RULED: 1430-e, INT-18). ONE
       seven-verb file-drop contract — list, stat, get, put, rename, delete,
       mkdir, with resumable transfers, size and time bounds and a bound root
       no path and no symlink escapes — specified once and INSTANTIATED by
       cx-platform/sftp (#1457) and cx-platform/ftp (#1458), so a connector
       feature written against a drop directory does not care which of the
       two carries it. It declares no surface — no namespace, no corpus, no
       code — so no `[module]` row can claim it, and it is declared here for
       the same reason composition.md is. A later object-storage kind
       instantiates the same page (1430-e's catalog, #1464). See
       spec/03-approved/platform/file_surface.md §0. ────────────────────── ]
  [catalog ring=1 group=platform repo=cx-platform-net path=deps/cx-platform-net/spec/03-approved/platform/file_surface.md]

  [; ── Ring 1 — cx-stdlib ───────────────────────────────────────────── ]
